## Solution

We seek an $(n-1)$-form $\omega$ on $\mathbb{R}^n$ with $d\omega = dx_1 \wedge \cdots \wedge dx_n$.

**Candidate.** Define

$$\omega = \frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}(-1)^{i-1}\,x_i\; dx_1 \wedge \cdots \wedge \widehat{dx_i} \wedge \cdots \wedge dx_n,$$

where $\widehat{dx_i}$ denotes omission of the factor $dx_i$.

**Verification.** Since $d^2=0$ and the $dx_j$ are closed, the only contribution to $d\omega$ comes from differentiating the coefficient $x_i$:

$$d\omega = \frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}(-1)^{i-1}\,dx_i \wedge dx_1 \wedge \cdots \wedge \widehat{dx_i} \wedge \cdots \wedge dx_n.$$

Fix $i$. In the $(n-1)$-form $dx_1 \wedge \cdots \wedge \widehat{dx_i} \wedge \cdots \wedge dx_n$, the factors $dx_1,\dots,dx_{i-1}$ appear before the (omitted) $i$-th slot. To place $dx_i$ into its natural $i$-th position, it must be commuted past these $i-1$ factors, incurring a sign $(-1)^{i-1}$:

$$dx_i \wedge dx_1 \wedge \cdots \wedge \widehat{dx_i} \wedge \cdots \wedge dx_n = (-1)^{i-1}\,dx_1 \wedge \cdots \wedge dx_n.$$

Hence each summand equals

$$\frac{1}{n}(-1)^{i-1}\cdot(-1)^{i-1}\,dx_1\wedge\cdots\wedge dx_n = \frac{1}{n}\,dx_1\wedge\cdots\wedge dx_n.$$

Summing over $i=1,\dots,n$ (there are $n$ identical terms):

$$d\omega = n\cdot\frac{1}{n}\,dx_1\wedge\cdots\wedge dx_n = dx_1\wedge\cdots\wedge dx_n.$$

Thus the required $(n-1)$-form is

$$\boxed{\;\omega = \frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}(-1)^{i-1}\,x_i\; dx_1 \wedge \cdots \wedge \widehat{dx_i} \wedge \cdots \wedge dx_n.\;}$$

